\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A scalar endomorphism with two conjugate values}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For an endomorphism of a single complex line, a unitary
change of that line acts on the source and target together, as
in the [Yukawa map](fdip-011M). Use two channels on this line
with values #{Y_1=\ui\bbI} and #{Y_2=\bbI}. Their
[ordered Gram pair](fdip-018P) is #{(\bbI,\bbI)}, unchanged by
coordinate conjugation. Conjugation takes the first channel
itself to #{-\ui\bbI}. Every linear unitary on the line commutes
with #{\ui\bbI}, so a simultaneous change of its source and
target cannot carry that endomorphism to #{-\ui\bbI}.}
